<p>
       It follows from $(ii)$ that the vertical maps in this diagram are Kan fibrations, so that (<a data-tag="03KM" href="/tag/03KM">11.23</a>) is a homotopy pullback diagram (Example <a data-tag="010U" href="/tag/010U">3.4.1.3</a>). Our inductive hypothesis guarantees that the Kan complexes $Y_{ \operatorname{sk}_{n-1}(S) }$ and $Y_{ \operatorname{sk}_{n-1}(T) }$ are contractible, so that the lower horizontal map is a homotopy equivalence. Applying Corollary <a data-tag="010Q" href="/tag/010Q">3.4.1.5</a>, we see that the map $Y_{S} \rightarrow Y_{T}$ is also a homotopy equivalence. We may therefore replace $S$ by $T$, and thereby reduce to the case where $S$ is a coproduct of simplices. Since the collection of contractible Kan complexes is closed under the formation of products (Remark <a data-tag="0193" href="/tag/0193">3.1.7.8</a>), we can use $(i)$ to further reduce to the special case where $S = \Delta ^ n$ is a standard simplex. Replacing $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ by the projection map $S \times _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow S$, we are reduced to proving Lemma <a data-tag="03KL" href="/tag/03KL">11.9.5.7</a> in the special case where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \Delta ^ n$ is a standard simplex. 
